    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d505">

                <pb facs="0925" n="879" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v11p1/pg879"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">772.00/5–2854: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dillon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi><anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d505-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">limited official
                            use</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-28T21:00:00+01:00">May 28, 1954—9 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4587. In discussion today with Emb officer, Basdevant FonOff stated that
                    Voizard’s statement following assassination of five French <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Colons</hi> in Kef region accurately reflects govt’s
                        attitude.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d505-f2"/> In statement Voizard declared
                    nothing would be neglected to discover and punish assassins and that all steps
                    would be taken effectively to protect Tunisians and French living in cities or
                    country against aggression by outlaws. Govt does not wish to give political
                    flavor to this attack or to suggest that it bears resemblance to terrorist
                    activities during Hauteclocque regime. <pb facs="0926" n="880" xml:id="pg880"/>
                    Basdevant implied FonOff regretted tone of residency statement on assassinations
                    and trying play it down, while recognizing it was issued at time when emotions
                    running high.</p>
                <p>FonOff has considered possibility that assassinations may have been reprisal for
                    killing of eight <hi rendition="#italic">fellagahs</hi> earlier in week but has
                    no specific evidence.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d505-f3"/> It is not inclined to link
                    act to Bourguiba’s transfer despite somewhat inflammatory nature of latter’s
                    statements to press. FonOff continues to believe direction and material support
                    for <hi rendition="#italic">fellagahs</hi> comes principally from abroad,
                    particularly Cairo and Libya. So far there is no evidence of Neo Destour
                    sponsorship.</p>
                <p>While vigorous efforts will continue be made to liquidate <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >fellagahs</hi>, no new measures of repression envisaged for Tunisians as
                    such.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d505-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d505-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was repeated to Tunis, Malta, and Rome for Maffitt.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d505-f2" n="2" xml:id="d505-f2-content">
                <p>Tunis telegram 88, May 28, reported the murder of five French colonists. The
                    assailants left a note on the bodies warning against French colonialism.
                    Despatch 255 from Tunis. June 1, transmitted a summary of Voizard’s speech
                    regarding the action to be taken by the French against terrorists. Documentation
                    is in Department of State file 772.00.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d505-f3" n="3" xml:id="d505-f3-content">
                <p>Tunis despatches 239, May 19, and 252, May 26, reported on <hi
                        rendition="#italic">fellagah</hi> activities and French countermeasures.
                    Documentation is in Department of State file 772.00.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
